>> Hello,
>>
>>
>>
>> The landlord has a right to enter your unit for the following reasons:
>>
>> *Landlord's right to enter rental unit*
>>
>> *54(1)  * A landlord shall not enter a rental unit occupied by a tenant
>> under a tenancy agreement except where
>>
>> (a) an emergency exists and entry to the rental unit is necessary;
>>
>> (b) the tenant consents to the entry;
>>
>> (c) the landlord shows the rental unit to a prospective tenant after
>> notice of termination is given under this Act but before the tenant has
>> vacated the unit;
>>
>> (d) the landlord inspects the rental unit on the day the tenant is
>> required to vacate the unit to complete a rental unit condition report o=
r
>> to determine if the tenant has fulfilled the tenant's obligations under
>> this Act and the tenancy agreement, and the inspection takes place at a
>> reasonable time;
>>
>> (e) the landlord in good faith believes that the rental unit has been
>> abandoned by the tenant and gives written notice to the tenant in
>> accordance with subsection (2);
>>
>> (f) the landlord gives written notice in accordance with subsection (3)
>> and entry is for one of the following purposes:
>>
>> (i) to perform an obligation of the landlord under this Act or the
>> tenancy agreement or to inspect the rental unit to determine if an
>> obligation should be performed,
>>
>> (ii) to complete a rental unit condition report if the tenant has
>> requested the landlord's consent to an assignment or subletting,
>>
>> (iii) to permit a mortgagee or insurer or prospective mortgagee or
>> insurer to inspect the unit when a mortgage or insurance coverage is bei=
ng
>> arranged or renewed on the residential complex, or
>>
>> (iv) to show the rental unit to prospective purchasers of the rental uni=
t
>> or residential complex;
>>
>> (g) the landlord enters the rental unit to provide tenant services to th=
e
>> tenant in accordance with the tenancy agreement.
>>
>>
>>
>>
>>
>> You will notice that renovations is not listed in that section of the
>> Act. There is a separate process for doing extensive renovations and tha=
t
>> process involves terminating your tenancy where you would have to move o=
ut
>> completely so that they can do the renovations. If you wanted to move ba=
ck
>> in after those renovations are completed you would have the right of fir=
st
>> refusal. The question becomes, is this an extensive renovation? If it wo=
uld
>> mean that you would be without water and maybe even electricity for some
>> extended period of time, it might qualify as that.
>>
>>
>>
>> If you refuse them entry to do this they may give you a warning letter
>> stating that you cannot refuse entry. If you continue to refuse entry af=
ter
>> that they could give you an eviction notice. If that happens you have a
>> right to dispute the notice and not move. They would more than likely
>> contact us to schedule a hearing about the eviction and at this hearing =
we
>> would then make a decision on whether you had a right to refuse and whet=
her
>> this was something the landlord had a right to do.
>>
>>
>>
>> We can also try to reach out to the landlord and ask them some questions
>> about this and determine whether or not they can do this now or after
>> terminating a tenancy. If you would like us to contact them please provi=
de
>> a contact and phone number for your landlord.
>>
>>
>>
>> Thanks.
>>
>>
>>
